# Confirm Before Depot / Git Restore Points

Before changing the Unity project back to a previous depot, Plastic, or git save point, **STOP and ask the user for confirmation**. Do not proceed until they explicitly approve in the current message.

## What counts as a restore

- `git checkout` / `git switch` to another branch or commit that changes project files
- `git restore`, `git reset`, or discarding work to match an older commit
- Plastic SCM / Unity Version Control revert, switch changeset, or load older depot save
- Loading or restoring a named checkpoint, backup branch, or recovery snapshot that undoes current work
- Checking out `main` (or any older tip) as “cleanup” when it would drop unmerged feature work

## Before asking, name both

1. **Save point** — exact commit hash, branch name, Plastic changeset / depot label, or backup branch (e.g. `5c81c1c82`, `fix/audit-bug-pass`, `backup/pre-audit-fixes`).
2. **Agent prompt timeline** — which conversation or work that checkpoint represents (e.g. “after inventory-drop unify”, “before audio mute fix”, “pre-settings modal rewrite”).

## Example ask

> About to restore to **`a049a27b4`** (`Backup: _Project state before audit bug fix pass`) — timeline: **before inventory-drop / audio / settings work on this chat**. Proceed?

## Switching to `main`

When the user asks to “go to main”, “switch to main”, or “cleanup onto main”:

1. Compare `main` to the current feature branch (or HEAD): `git log --oneline main..HEAD` / `git rev-list --count main..HEAD`.
2. If `main` is **behind** unmerged feature work, **WARN** that checking out `main` will look like a revert / lost progress.
3. Offer options and wait for explicit choice:
   - **Merge first** — merge the feature branch into `main` (no force push), then stay on / switch to `main`
   - **Stay on feature branch** — keep working where the latest state lives
   - **Checkout main anyway** — only after the user confirms they accept losing unmerged tip work in the working tree view
4. Never treat “go to main” as permission to discard or hard-reset feature commits.

## Working scene (never silent Recovery)

- Prefer the real playable scene: **`Assets/_Project/Scenes/Dark Matter Genesis v1.6.2.unity`** (or the current numbered Genesis scene if renamed later).
- **Never** load `Assets/_Recovery/*` as the working scene after a crash, domain reload, or branch switch without asking.
- Crash dumps under `_Recovery/` are forensic only — not the intended project state.
- If Unity opens a Recovery scene, stop, tell the user, and offer to load Dark Matter Genesis v1.6.2 instead.

## Do not

- Silently roll back Unity or the working tree to an older save
- Checkout `main` or older commits/Plastic changesets as a “cleanup” without asking
- Treat crash-recovery scenes under `Assets/_Recovery/` as the intended project state without asking
- Force-push or hard-reset unless the user explicitly requested that destructive action **and** confirmed the named save point
- Use Unity Version Control / Plastic auto-switch or “get latest” in a way that silently reverts the working copy to an older changeset without confirmation

## Version Control note

This project uses **git** as source of truth (`VersionControlSettings` = Visible Meta Files). Do not enable Plastic/UVCS workspace switching that can pull an older depot tip over the git working tree unless the user explicitly asks and confirms the target changeset.
